.SH NAME
archive\-project \- move stale code projects from ~/Code/ to the NAS

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B archive-project
[\fB\-n\fR | \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fB\-y\fR | \fB\-\-yes\fR]
[\fB\-\-keep\-deps\fR]
[\fB\-\-months=\fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-man\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B archive-project
recursively scans
.I ~/Code/
for git repositories and offers to move ones that are
.B clean
and
.B old
to the NAS at
.IR /Volumes/media/code/archived/ ,
preserving the original folder layout. The original directory structure
under
.I ~/Code/
is reproduced under the archive root, so
.I ~/Code/MyOrg/foo
becomes
.IR /Volumes/media/code/archived/MyOrg/foo .

.PP
The script is deliberately conservative: it never modifies a project with
uncommitted work, never descends into a repo to grab a vendored sub-repo,
and never deletes anything from the SSD until the destination on the NAS
is fully written. A NAS dropout mid-move cannot leave you half-archived.

.SH DECISION LOGIC
A project becomes a candidate only when
.B all three
of these are true:

.TP
.B 1. It's a regular git repo
The directory contains a
.I .git/
subdirectory. Worktrees and submodules
.RI ( .git
is a regular file, not a directory) are skipped because moving them out
of place would break their parent's linkage.

.TP
.B 2. The working tree is fully clean
.B git status --porcelain
prints nothing. No staged, unstaged, or untracked changes. Projects with
any uncommitted work are
.B always
skipped, regardless of age \(em you might be mid-thought, not done.

.TP
.B 3. The last commit is older than the threshold
The timestamp of the most recent commit must be older than
.B ARCHIVE_AFTER_MONTHS
months (default
.BR 1 ).
Change the constant at the top of the script for a permanent change, or
pass
.B \-\-months=\fIN
for a one-off run.

.PP
A repo nested inside another repo (e.g., a vendored library with its own
.IR .git/ )
is skipped \(em it belongs to the ancestor repo and will move with it
when the ancestor is archived.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what
.I would
be archived without touching anything. The full report (skipped
projects, candidates, intended destinations) prints exactly as in a real
run, but nothing is stripped, copied, or removed.

.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Auto-confirm every candidate. Dirty / fresh / nested / non-repo projects
are still skipped \(em \fB\-y\fR is yes-to-all for
.IR candidates ,
not yes-to-everything. Without this flag, the script asks
.B [y/N/q]
per candidate.

.TP
.B \-\-keep\-deps
Do not strip
.IR node_modules ,
.IR Pods ,
.IR .gradle ,
etc. before moving. By default these are blown away because they're huge
and trivially regenerated. Use this when you specifically want the
build state preserved \(em e.g., archiving a project at a known-working
toolchain version.

.TP
\fB\-\-months=\fIN\fR
Treat any project whose last commit is older than
.I N
months as a candidate. Overrides the
.B ARCHIVE_AFTER_MONTHS
constant for this run only. Must be a positive integer.

.TP
.B \-\-man
Open this man page in your pager.

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The script ships with these defaults at the top of the file. Edit them in
.I bin/archive-project
for permanent changes:

.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ARCHIVE_AFTER_MONTHS=1
CODE_DIR="$HOME/Code"
ARCHIVE_DIR="/Volumes/media/code/archived"
NAS_MOUNT="/Volumes/media"
LOG="$HOME/.archive-project/archive.log"

STRIP_DIRS=(
  node_modules .next .nuxt .svelte-kit .astro .turbo .vite
  .parcel-cache .cache Pods DerivedData .gradle .expo
  .expo-shared .dart_tool __pycache__ .pytest_cache
  .mypy_cache .ruff_cache
)
STRIP_FILES=(
  .flutter-plugins .flutter-plugins-dependencies
)
.fi
.RE

.PP
.B STRIP_DIRS
entries are matched by
.B name
anywhere in the tree, not by relative path. A monorepo's nested
.I node_modules
and a React Native app's
.I ios/Pods
both get cleaned without needing per-framework path patterns.
.B build/ ,
.BR dist/ ,
.BR out/ ,
.BR target/ ,
and
.B venv/
are deliberately left alone \(em their names are too generic to delete
safely by default.

.SH MOVE SEMANTICS
The "move" is a two-step copy-then-remove, not a
.BR mv :

.IP "1." 4
.B cp -RXp src dst
\(em recursive, no extended attributes (so SMB doesn't choke on
.B com.apple.provenance
and friends), preserves mode and mtime.
.IP "2." 4
If the copy completes, the SSD source is removed with
.BR "rm -rf" .
.IP "3." 4
If the copy fails, the partial destination on the NAS is wiped and the
SSD source is left intact for retry.

.PP
The result: a NAS dropout or full-disk error never strands you with half
a project on each side. The source survives until the destination is
whole.

.PP
If the destination name already exists at the archive root, a date suffix
is appended:
.IR foo " \[->] " foo-YYYYMMDD " \[->] " foo-YYYYMMDD-1 " \[->] " ...

.SH PROCESSING ORDER
Candidates are sorted by relative path, so outer projects are offered
before inner ones. If you accept the outer, the inner candidate's source
ceases to exist on its turn and it silently skips. This matches the
mental model: when an org-style container is archived, its children
travel inside it, not separately.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All candidates either archived or declined cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
At least one move failed; see the log.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage (unknown flag, non-numeric
.BR \-\-months ).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/archive-project
The script itself. Edit the config block at the top for permanent changes.
.TP
.I ~/.archive-project/archive.log
Append-only log of every archive action.
.TP
.I /Volumes/media/code/archived/
Default destination. Created on first run.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview what would happen, no changes:
.B archive-project --dry-run

.TP
Run interactively at the default 1-month threshold:
.B archive-project

.TP
Be stricter for a one-off run \(em only touch projects untouched for 6+ months:
.B archive-project --months=6

.TP
Bulk-archive everything that qualifies, no per-project prompts:
.B archive-project -y

.TP
Archive a project without stripping its
.IR node_modules :
.B archive-project --keep-deps

.SH NOTES
.B archive-project
requires the NAS to be mounted at
.IR /Volumes/media .
The
.I .inetloc
Finder login item handles this at login; if the mount is missing, the
script exits early with an instruction for how to remount.

.PP
Parent directories that become empty after a successful archive are
.B not
auto-removed. The script prints a note listing them so you can
.B rmdir
them by hand if you want \(em that choice stays with you.

